592186050837 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050837 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050837 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050837 #edb/ref 1031 "Distance Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050837 #edb/ref 1033 1392 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050837 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6a847353-9f59-5913-9053-7a03efa215fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161771 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8156" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161771 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n![](images/fb/fb428c39bbc03be798bca2463658676de5c684396001cf547e9395352f456bc9.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161771 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c68d70a2-e4f8-5f08-8220-3df420a1e199" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161771 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21},$ we obtain $R \\neq R^T.$\n![](images/3f/3f91ec40e717eac93bb38f9c13f7b1700e84ac109ccceaffbb8473ba8d6b4e34.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161816 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161816 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+A^T$ is always symmetric.\n\nII. The difference between two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\nIII. The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is symmetric." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161816 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b159d52a-a436-5743-9151-2e147364a41d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161816 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the sum or difference between two symmetric matrices is symmetric.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n![](images/81/812e9888b920e0338fd72116464b8b96c8c272f95f180cd46aa3f1db9afac8d2.svg)\nTheir product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161979 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186162613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186161979 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 175921