2859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186162925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1045 "Symmetric Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186162847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162214 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f83b8c51-727f-5eb0-9f6f-bcc3b133cfd6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449451 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449452 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162676 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449453 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162676 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96046" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162676 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162676 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $A$ is *any* matrix, then $A{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- **II.** The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- **III.** The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162676 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8be226ee-498f-567c-82d1-206ed205336b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162676 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A{A}^{T})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =A{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162694 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477721 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162694 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477722 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162694 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186477723 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162694 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162694 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162694 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then $A-{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric.\n- **II.** The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- **III.** If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186162694 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2943df26-2edc-5f92-bec7-1493042a2419" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162694 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, $A-{A}^{T}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{A}^{T} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162847 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162847 #edb/ref 1061 30531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162847 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c19c64f-0597-5273-8ff3-50729d7a67fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162847 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163222 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162859 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162859 #edb/ref 1061 30550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162859 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2c61bc7-19cc-5e6a-a533-0450e49aa5dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162859 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186161979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162924 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186162925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162924 #edb/ref 1061 31545 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162924 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a119eca9-e695-53d9-9049-eef3212a6e22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162924 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162925 #edb/ref 1061 31546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162925 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dd82fa90-8f6a-5c31-a6bd-faa30ff2ee22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186162925 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163222 #edb/ref 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is **symmetric** if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA^T = A\n$$\n\nEquivalently, an $n\\times n$ matrix is symmetric if $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $1 \\leq i,j\\leq n.$\n\nIf $A$ is symmetric, this really means that it is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/0f/0f62a89f8da1ab7bfb86d46fddb441e95e5e3a9554e5a88662691b4df40e19af.svg)\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$ \n\nOn the other hand, the matrix \n\n![](images/78/780de3c3f66b1037e4152224f4aa98b65b8b1b859adfe7c3881bb830d7eecd66.svg)\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163222 #edb/ref 1065 4880 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163222 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1fe9d8bc-6133-5ab4-8b4f-af2b40817e02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163222 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163254 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if \n![](images/e4/e41d28b6c6562ada530024fe6ab29cc16447dce14b2f741b8b35758618c78103.svg)\nthen the product $A\\cdot B$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163254 #edb/ref 1065 5043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163254 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c20572b7-1e75-5be2-a39c-b2327caff234" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163254 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of Symmetric Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357611 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470431 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186357611 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470432 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186357611 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186470433 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186357611 #edb/ref 1069 "q-93985" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357611 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357611 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186357611 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "225f8bdf-5da4-5137-973e-d6466e5f7f7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186357611 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{12}\\ne {p}_{21},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q$ only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452979 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452980 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452981 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96589" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n\n- **II.** The product of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n\n- **III.** If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6c182c5-819c-5206-a031-9309a3557f8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360684 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the symmetric matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any non-singular symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{-1})^{T} & =({A}^{T})^{-1} \\\\  & ={A}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452985 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452986 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186452987 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366739 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96594" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366739 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366739 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- **II.** If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n- **III.** If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186366739 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df6e5142-313f-5de3-a45d-4da4b737f0bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366739 #edb/ref 1074 "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the non-singular matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{-1}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & ={A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186425147 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186425155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218642